              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Figure 2: (left) Scaled Dot-Product Attention. (right) Multi-Head Attention consists of several attention layers running in parallel."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.398,
            0.826,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "of the values, where the weight assigned to each value is computed by a compatibility function of the query with the corresponding key."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.442,
            0.434,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.2.1 Scaled Dot-Product Attention"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.465,
            0.827,
            0.522
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We call our particular attention \"Scaled Dot-Product Attention\" (Figure 2). The input consists of queries and keys of dimension "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d_{k}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and values of dimension "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d_{v}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . We compute the dot products of the query with all keys, divide each by "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { d _ { k } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and apply a softmax function to obtain the weights on the values."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.528,
            0.826,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In practice, we compute the attention function on a set of queries simultaneously, packed together into a matrix Q. The keys and values are also packed together into matrices K and V. We compute the matrix of outputs as:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.585,
            0.825,
            0.62
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_0_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "Attention (Q, K, V) =  softmax (\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}})V\\tag{1}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.629,
            0.827,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The two most commonly used attention functions are additive attention [2], and dot-product (multi-plicative) attention. Dot-product attention is identical to our algorithm, except for the scaling factor of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d_{k}}}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Additive attention computes the compatibility function using a feed-forward network with a single hidden layer. While the two are similar in theoretical complexity, dot-product attention is much faster and more space-efficient in practice, since it can be implemented using highly optimized matrix multiplication code."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.721,
            0.827,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "While for small values of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d_{k}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the two mechanisms perform similarly, additive attention outperforms dot product attention without scaling for larger values of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d_{k}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " [3]. We suspect that for large values of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d_{k},"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the dot products grow large in magnitude, pushing the softmax function into regions where it has extremely small gradients 4. To counteract this effect, we scale the dot products by "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d_{k}}}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.795,
            0.38,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.2.2 Multi-Head Attention"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.818,
            0.827,
            0.876
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Instead of performing a single attention function with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d _ { \\mathrm { m o d e l } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " -dimensional keys, values and queries, we found it beneficial to linearly project the queries, keys and values h times with different, learned linear projections to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d _ { k } ,   d _ { k }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d_{v}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " dimensions, respectively. On each of these projected versions of queries, keys and values we then perform the attention function in parallel, yielding "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d_{v}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " -dimensional"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.885,
            0.825,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4To illustrate why the dot products get large, assume that the components of q and k are independent random variables with mean 0 and variance 1. Then their dot product, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\begin{array} { r } { q \\cdot k = \\sum _ { i = 1 } ^ { d _ { k } } q _ { i } k _ { i } } \\end{array}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , has mean 0 and variance "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d_{k}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 12,
